@@s001 Scala in ten minutes
@@s002 dato:local names,ora:the line that matters,ok:value of the block
@@s003 A block after <code>val area =</code>: two local names and a final expression.
@@s004 Inside the block two names are born: base = 4 and altezza = 3.
@@s005 The <b>value</b> of the block is its last line: base * altezza. No <code>return</code>.
@@s006 4 * 3 = <b>12</b>: area is worth 12.
@@s007 Outside the block base and altezza no longer exist: only area remains. In Scala everything is an expression with a value, even a block or an if.
@@s008 dato:public member,no:private (only inside),ok:successful call
@@s009 ora:current line,ok:allowed in the course,no:forbidden
@@s010 dato:your file,ora:command,ok:everything passes,no:error
@@s011 no:error,ok:correct,dato:where it really is
@@s012 Scala in ten minutes
@@s013 The words you find in every file of the course: <code>package</code>, <code>object</code>, <code>def</code>, <code>val</code>, <code>if</code>. Plus the <b>scala-cli</b> commands to compile and run the tests.
@@s014 The week 1 file, line by line
@@s015 This is the beginning of <code>01-intro/Exercises.scala</code>. It contains almost all the basic syntax.
@@s016 01-intro/Exercises.scala
@@s017 A whole file. Press <b>Next →</b>: each step lights up the lines it talks about.
@@s018 <code>package</code> says in which "logical folder" the code lives, as in Java. The full name of everything that follows starts with <code>adpro.intro</code>.
@@s019 <code>object</code> creates a container with <b>a single instance</b>. It is used as a namespace: from outside you write <code>MyModule.abs(42)</code>. It is like a Java class with only <code>static</code> methods.
@@s020 The <b>colon</b> at the end of the line opens a block. In Scala 3 the block is made by the <b>indentation</b>, not by braces.
@@s021 A function: name, parameters with their type, return type, <code>=</code>. After the equals sign comes the body.
@@s022 <code>if … then … else</code> is an <b>expression</b>: it has a value. Here the value of the whole <code>if</code> is the result of <code>abs</code>. In Java it would be <code>n &lt; 0 ? -n : n</code>.
@@s023 There is no <code>return</code>: the result of a function is simply the value of the last expression of the body.
@@s024 <code>private</code>: it can be used only inside <code>MyModule</code>.
@@s025 A string that starts with <code>s</code> can contain values: <code>${…}</code> is replaced with the value of the expression inside.
@@s026 <code>val</code> gives a name to a value <b>that no longer changes</b>. Always use it.
@@s027 <code>var</code> is a modifiable variable. In the course it is <b>forbidden</b>: the slide puts it here only to show it. <code>Option[Int]</code> and <code>None</code> you see in week 3.
@@s028 <code>@main</code> marks the starting point of the program. <code>Unit</code> means "no useful result", like <code>void</code>.
@@s029 <code>-</code> is also a <b>method</b>. <code>magic - 84</code> is just a convenient way to write <code>magic.-(84)</code>. The <code>assert</code> checks that they are equal.
@@s030 <code>end MyModule</code> closes the block explicitly. It is optional, it helps in long files.
@@s031 package, object, private, @main
@@s032 Everything is an expression
@@s033 In Java you separate <b>statements</b> (they do something) and <b>expressions</b> (they are worth something). In Scala almost everything is worth something, even a whole block.
@@s034 What is a block worth?
@@s035 An indented block after <code>val area =</code>. Inside there are two local names.
@@s036 The value of the block is the <b>last line</b>.
@@s037 The names are replaced with their values.
@@s038 <code>base</code> and <code>altezza</code> outside the block no longer exist. Only <code>area</code> remains.
@@s039 The value of a block
@@s040 <code>val</code>, <code>var</code>, <code>def</code>: three ways to give a name
@@s041 Written
@@s042 When it is computed
@@s043 Can it be changed?
@@s044 In the course
@@s045 <code>val x = …</code>
@@s046 immediately, once
@@s047 no
@@s048 <span class="t-ok">yes, always</span>
@@s049 <code>def x = …</code>
@@s050 every time you write <code>x</code>
@@s051 no
@@s052 <span class="t-ok">yes</span>
@@s053 <code>lazy val x = …</code>
@@s054 at first use, then remembers
@@s055 no
@@s056 <span class="t-ok">yes</span> (week 4)
@@s057 <code>var x = …</code>
@@s058 immediately
@@s059 yes, with <code>x = …</code>
@@s060 <span class="t-no">forbidden</span>
@@s061 When the value is computed
@@s062 Running the code with scala-cli
@@s063 All commands are run <b>from the week's folder</b> and end with a <b>dot</b>, which means "this folder".
@@s064 Command
@@s065 What it does
@@s066 <code>scala-cli compile .</code>
@@s067 Just compiles. Useful to see the errors.
@@s068 <code>scala-cli test .</code>
@@s069 Compiles and runs the tests of <code>Exercises.test.scala</code>.
@@s070 <code>scala-cli test . -w</code>
@@s071 As above, but reruns the tests <b>on every save</b>. The recommended way of working.
@@s072 <code>scala-cli repl .</code>
@@s073 Opens the REPL with your code loaded, to try by hand.
@@s074 <code>scala-cli run . -M adpro.intro.printAbs</code>
@@s075 Runs the indicated <code>@main</code> (<code>-M</code> is needed if there is more than one).
@@s076 <code>scala-cli clean .</code>
@@s077 Deletes the generated files. Try this if "it worked before and now it does not".
@@s078 In the REPL the functions must be called with the full name, or imported:
@@s079 test -w, repl, clean
@@s080 Typical errors
@@s081 
@@s082 It compiles, but it is forbidden
@@s083 <code>def square(n: Int): Int = return n * n</code> compiles without errors. But <code>return</code> is in the list of constructs forbidden in the exercises and at the exam: write only <code>n * n</code>. See <a href="../esame/regole.html">Rules and prohibitions</a>.
@@s084 value … is not a member of …
